{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pct-02-parts-wholes-and-rates",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Finding a Percentage, a Part, or a Whole",
    "description": "Calculate percentage amounts and solve for an unknown rate, part, or original total.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the model step by step, explain why each operation matches the wording, show intermediate arithmetic, check units and magnitude, then practice transfer with specific remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 11 “Percentage,” printed pages 308–345. Approved scope: Teach part = rate/100 × whole and derive its three rearrangements: find the part, find the rate, or find the whole. Solve missing-value questions, repeated percentage-of calculations, and problems where the remainder is a stated percent. Show the base of each percent explicitly and use fractions or multipliers to avoid premature rounding. Include checks by substituting the result into the original relation and estimating whether the answer's size is plausible."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Finding a Percentage, a Part, or a Whole: Calculate percentage amounts and solve for an unknown rate, part, or original total. This lesson builds the method from its meaning, works through the reasoning and checks, then practices it with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The core relationship is part=(rate/100)×whole. It names three roles: the percentage rate, the comparison whole, and the resulting part. Labeling these roles prevents using the wrong denominator.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To find a part, convert the rate to a multiplier. 18% of 250=(18/100)×250=45. Estimate first: about one fifth of 250 is 50, so 45 is plausible.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To find what percent one quantity is of another, divide the part by the stated whole, then multiply by 100. If 42 students out of 60 pass, 42/60×100%=70%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To find the whole, divide the known part by the decimal rate. If 15% of a number is 36, then 0.15N=36 and N=36/0.15=240. Multiplying by 15% again would move in the wrong direction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a missing rate, isolate it: p% of 80=18 means (p/100)×80=18, so p=18×100/80=22.5. Substitute: 22.5% of 80 is 18.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Nested percentages multiply. 40% of 30% of N is 0.4×0.3×N=0.12N, so the result is 12% of N, not 70%. Each “of” adds a multiplication step.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When the remainder is stated, use complements. If 32% of a sum remains as cash, then 68% was spent. If the spent amounts total 77,384, solve 0.68N=77,384.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A percentage of a percentage changes the base at each stage only if the story says the second rate applies to the remainder. Write the current amount after every step instead of silently using the original base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A quantity may be a fraction of a total before a percentage is applied. If 40% of 3/5 of N is 72, then 0.4×0.6N=72, so N=300.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For small rates, use exact fractions where possible. 12.5%=1/8, 20%=1/5, and 0.25%=1/400. This often makes missing-whole calculations faster and more accurate.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "An answer can be checked by reversing the calculation. For a missing whole, multiply the result by the original rate; for a missing rate, recompute the part and compare it with the given amount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the rate exceeds 100%, the part exceeds the whole. This is possible for growth, accumulated amounts, and ratios; do not reject an answer simply because it is above the base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep units alongside the whole and part. If a percentage produces rupees, kilograms, or marks, the result retains that unit. A bare number can conceal a unit mismatch.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The order of operations is part of the model: identify which amount is the whole, turn each percent into a multiplier, multiply in the stated sequence, and only then isolate the unknown.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Before accepting a value, check the equation in its original wording and compare the result with a rough estimate. A correct algebraic manipulation can still answer the wrong “whole.”",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Apply the method to this new case. Before choosing, identify the comparison base and state the operation that matches the wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
